// src/comdraw/examples/affine.comt -- five commands, one operation
//
// comdraw has five commands that move a graphic around: move, scale, rotate,
// fliph and flipv.  They are not five operations.  Every one of them is a
// single affine transform composed onto the graphic, and this script proves
// it by doing each one both ways and comparing the resulting matrix.
//
// The named command runs on one rect; trans(cv m :apply) runs the equivalent
// matrix on an identical one.  When the two matrices agree, the named command
// was that matrix all along.
//
//   comdraw -runfile src/comdraw/examples/affine.comt
//   run("src/comdraw/examples/affine.comt")
//
// Why it matters beyond the arithmetic: a distributed drawing editor has to
// send these across a link.  Five commands means five relays; one affine
// means one.  What the compression costs is spelled out at the end.
//
// Note the comparisons are assigned before being printed.  Writing
// print("..." ma mb (ma==mb)) instead would parse `mb (...)` as a CALL of mb
// -- a symbol followed directly by a paren group is a function call, whatever
// the symbol holds -- and print would silently receive one argument short.

// a fresh rect, alone on the canvas, so every case starts from the same place
fresh=func(each(delete($$select(:all))); rect(0,0,40,20) :posteval)

// --- move -------------------------------------------------------------
// pure translation: the one case that needs no pivot
a=fresh(); select(a); move(10 20); ma=trans(a)
b=fresh(); trans(b 1,0,0,1,10,20 :apply); mb=trans(b)
same=(ma==mb)
print("move(10 20)\n  named   %v\n  affine  %v\n  same    %v\n\n" ma mb same)

// --- scale ------------------------------------------------------------
// scaling happens about the graphic's centre, not the origin, so the affine
// has to carry the pivot: c - c*s on each axis
b=fresh(); c=center(b); cx=at(c 0); cy=at(c 1)
a=fresh(); select(a); scale(2 3); ma=trans(a)
b=fresh(); trans(b 2,0,0,3, cx*(1-2), cy*(1-3) :apply); mb=trans(b)
same=(ma==mb)
print("scale(2 3)\n  named   %v\n  affine  %v\n  same    %v\n\n" ma mb same)

// --- fliph ------------------------------------------------------------
// a horizontal flip is scale(-1,1) about that same centre
a=fresh(); select(a); fliph(); ma=trans(a)
b=fresh(); trans(b -1,0,0,1, 2*cx, 0 :apply); mb=trans(b)
same=(ma==mb)
print("fliph()\n  named   %v\n  affine  %v\n  same    %v\n\n" ma mb same)

// --- flipv ------------------------------------------------------------
a=fresh(); select(a); flipv(); ma=trans(a)
b=fresh(); trans(b 1,0,0,-1, 0, 2*cy :apply); mb=trans(b)
same=(ma==mb)
print("flipv()\n  named   %v\n  affine  %v\n  same    %v\n\n" ma mb same)

// --- rotate -----------------------------------------------------------
// rotation about the centre: the usual cos/sin block, offset by c - c*R.
//
// This is the one case that does not compare equal, and the reason is worth
// seeing rather than hiding.  Unidraw turns the angle into a matrix in single
// precision, so its cos(90) is -4.37e-08; comterp's cos() is double, so the
// same zero comes out as 6.12e-17.  Both are zero to their own precision and
// the graphic lands in the same place -- the matrices agree everywhere the
// arithmetic is exact and differ only where each was rounding its own zero.
// So compare within a tolerance here, and print the actual gap.
rad=degtorad(90); co=cos(rad); si=sin(rad)
a=fresh(); select(a); rotate(90); ma=trans(a)
b=fresh(); trans(b co,si,0-si,co, cx-cx*co+cy*si, cy-cx*si-cy*co :apply); mb=trans(b)
gap=0; i=0
while(i<6
  e=at(ma i)-at(mb i);
  if(e<0 :then e=0-e);
  if(e>gap :then gap=e);
  i=i+1)
same=(gap<0.000001)
print("rotate(90)\n  named   %v\n  affine  %v\n  same    %v (largest difference %v)\n\n" ma mb same gap)

// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
// What the five commands carry that the matrix does not:
//
//   move    nothing -- a translation is a translation
//   scale   the centre to scale about
//   fliph   that centre, with the factors fixed at -1,1
//   flipv   that centre, with the factors fixed at 1,-1
//   rotate  that centre, and the angle in degrees
//
// The pivot is the whole difference.  Each named command works it out from
// the graphic's own bounds, which is why "flip this" is a shorter thing to
// say than the matrix it turns into.  Compressing them to one affine is
// lossless for the RESULT and lossy for the INTENT: the matrix cannot say it
// was a rotation of 90 degrees, only where everything ended up.
//
// Which is the real question for a shared editing history -- a log reading
// "rotate 90" is worth more to a person than one reading six floats, even
// though both put the graphic in the same place.

exit
